libview - shared 3D view math for radioglobe and claudegraph
============================================================

libview is a small, pure-math library holding the code that
radioglobe (/usr/dave/work/radioglobe) and claudegraph
(/usr/dave/work/claude9/claudegraph.c) had each grown their own
diverging copy of.  It owns only numbers: no draw.h, no event.h,
no locking, no I/O.  The caller owns the event loop, the
projection style (radioglobe's orthographic disc vs claudegraph's
perspective), display locking, and rendering.

The contract is view.h.  It requires u.h and libc.h to be
included first (double, ulong, frand, sqrt, ...).

Contents
--------
  v3.c      Vec3 helpers: v3/v3add/v3sub/v3scale/v3dot/v3len and
            v3rand (uniform-direction vector, uses frand()).
            Ported from claudegraph's vadd/vsub/vmul/vdot/rndvec.
            Also v3cross (right-handed cross product) and v3unit
            (unit vector, no zero-length guard), added for any
            future arbitrary eye/center/up camera built the same
            way orbit.c's viewbasis() builds its yaw/pitch one --
            neither consumer uses them yet.

  orbit.c   viewbasis() -- the orbit-camera screen basis
            (ex/ey screen right/up, ez toward the viewer),
            identical to claudegraph's setview() and radioglobe's
            viewbasis() with yaw==lon, pitch==lat -- plus the
            Orbit inertial-drag state machine: orbitinit,
            orbitdown (grab stops spin), orbitmove (wrap-safe
            velocity across the +-180 seam), orbitup, orbittick
            (friction + vmin cutoff), orbitzoom, orbitnorm.

            Orbit.frictionref makes the inertial decay depend on
            elapsed time rather than on orbittick() call count:
            set it to the caller's nominal tick length and pass
            the real elapsed ms, and orbittick subdivides into
            steps of at most that length, so a late tick applies
            exactly the motion its interval deserves.  It
            defaults to 0, which selects the original behavior
            (one friction application per call, whatever ms says)
            so existing callers are bit-for-bit unaffected;
            radioglobe opts in because its per-pixel solid-earth
            render can make a frame outlast the tick interval,
            which otherwise made the spin lag real time.

  spring.c  springdefaults + springstep, a force-directed graph
            layout: origin gravity, pairwise repulsion, edge
            springs, damped integration with a per-step speed cap
            and a |pos| clamp.  Faithful port of claudegraph's
            physstep() over Sbody pointers and an Sedge list.

  viewtest.c  Standalone test binary (no display) exercising the
              math; one PASS/FAIL line per check, exits nil iff
              all pass.

Consumers
---------
Two programs link this library:
  - claudegraph (/usr/dave/work/claude9), perspective projection.
  - radioglobe  (/usr/dave/work/radioglobe), orthographic globe.
Neither claude9fs, mkcoast, mkstations nor the other radioglobe
helpers link it.

Building
--------
    mk            build libview.a$O and viewtest
    mk clean      remove objects and outputs
    mk install    copy libview.a$O to $home/lib, view.h to $home/include

The archive is libview.a$O (e.g. libview.a7 on arm64); the $O
suffix disambiguates architecture within one flat directory, the
same way $home/bin/$cputype disambiguates binaries, so install
does not need a per-objtype subdirectory (unlike /$objtype/lib).
This follows the precedent set by libao (/usr/dave/work/ao/libao),
which installs libao.a and its headers to the same two directories.

Linking it into a consumer
--------------------------
The two current consumers (claudegraph, radioglobe) deliberately
do NOT use the installed copy yet: both are under active
development alongside libview, and pointing at the work tree
directly -- with a virtual rule that reruns mk there -- means a
consumer rebuild always picks up the latest library source
without a separate "mk install" step in between:

    LIBVIEW=/usr/dave/work/libview/libview.a$O
    CFLAGS=$CFLAGS -I/usr/dave/work/libview

    theprogram: $OFILES $LIBVIEW
        $LD -o $target $OFILES $LIBVIEW

    $LIBVIEW:V:
        @{cd /usr/dave/work/libview && mk}

A future or external consumer that doesn't share this source tree
(e.g. after libview is pushed to its own repo) should instead
build against the installed copy:

    LIBVIEW=$home/lib/libview.a$O
    CFLAGS=$CFLAGS -I$home/include

    theprogram: $OFILES $LIBVIEW
        $LD -o $target $OFILES $LIBVIEW

In that mode the consumer is responsible for running
"mk install" in libview after pulling a change; there is no
auto-rebuild rule for an installed library, same as any other
system library.

Running the tests
-----------------
    mk viewtest
    ./viewtest

viewtest calls srand(1) first, so its output is deterministic.
It prints one "PASS name" or "FAIL name: detail" line per check
and exits nil only if every check passed.
